@@ -70,6 +70,22 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
 
+Low level output
+----------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.out` method is a lower-level alternative to print and log. It writes its objects as plain text, joined by ``sep`` and followed by ``end``. Here's an example::
+
+    >>> console.out("[bold]Hello[/bold]", 1, sep=", ")
+
+It does the following:
+
+* Converts each object to a string and joins them with ``sep``. It does not pretty print containers.
+* Does not wrap, crop, or apply markup. Markup-like text and emoji codes are written literally.
+* Applies highlighting and an optional basic ``style``, like print does. ``highlight`` defaults to ``True``; set ``highlight=False`` to turn highlighting off for a call, or ``highlight=None`` to use the console default.
+
+Output from ``out`` goes through the same path as print, so it respects render hooks, capturing, and recording.
+
+
